{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127597",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}})\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(q(t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{6t}{{(1+3{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(t)+\\frac{1}{1+3{t}^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(t) \\\\  \\\\ {T}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{6(1)}{{(1+3(1)^{2})}^{2}}\\cdot q(1)+\\frac{1}{1+3(1)^{2}}\\cdot {q}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+\\frac{1}{4}⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-3⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩+2⟨0,\\sqrt{6},6⟩) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\sqrt{24} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${T}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve at the point where $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(1)}{‖{T}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{8}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when we find the scalar product of $T$ and $N$ at the point where $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1)\\cdot N(1) & =\\frac{1}{4}q(1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}⟨-3,-\\sqrt{6},3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(1\\cdot (-3)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot (-\\sqrt{6})+3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\sqrt{6}}(-3-6+9) \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
